[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Declarative vs. Scripted Pipeline Syntax and Differences in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins Pipeline allows users to define continuous delivery pipelines as code. When setting up a Jenkinsfile, you generally choose between two syntax models: Declarative and Scripted. Understanding the differences between these two approaches is crucial for designing maintainable and scalable CI/CD workflows."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Overview of Declarative Pipeline"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Declarative Pipeline is a relatively recent addition to Jenkins Continuous Delivery (CD) feature set. It provides a rich, predefined framework for defining pipelines, making it easier to write and read code for standard deployment processes."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Built on top of the Pipeline subsystem, offering a structured and pre-defined syntax."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Designed to make writing continuous delivery pipelines straightforward and accessible."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enforces a strict hierarchy using specific blocks like pipeline {}, agent {}, stages {}, and step {}."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building the application...'
            }
        }
        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                echo 'Running tests...'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"Overview of Scripted Pipeline"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Scripted Pipeline, also known as traditional pipeline, is built on top of the Groovy language. It provides a more imperative programming model, giving developers complete control and flexibility over the execution flow."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Written as a general-purpose Groovy script."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Offers immense flexibility and extensibility for complex, non-standard pipeline requirements."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Requires a deeper understanding of Groovy, including exception handling and object-oriented programming concepts."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"node {
    stage('Build') {
        echo 'Building the application...'
    }
    stage('Test') {
        echo 'Running tests...'
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Syntax Structure: Declarative uses a strict block-based structure (pipeline {}), whereas Scripted uses a free-form Groovy script structure (node {})."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Error Handling: Declarative has built-in directives like post {} for cleanup and failure handling, while Scripted relies heavily on standard Groovy try-catch-finally blocks."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ease of Use: Declarative is designed for beginners and teams looking for standard CI/CD practices. Scripted targets advanced users who need complex logic, loops, and custom shared libraries."},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing between Declarative and Scripted pipelines depends on your team's familiarity with Groovy and the complexity of your deployment workflows. For most modern use cases, the Declarative Pipeline is recommended due to its readability, built-in validation, and structured error management."}]
